// arg_temp_rooted.sf — an object constructed as argument 0 must survive argument 1
// allocating. BUGS #162, now FIXED; this is its regression test and it is an
// ordinary member of the pass suite. It spent one commit in run_tests.sh's
// KNOWN_FAIL table, where its failure read as `xfail`; the fix turned that into an
// `xpass`, which reddens the suite, and the entry was dropped in the same change.
//
// The shape is the ordinary one — `f(Box(7), something_that_allocates())` — not a
// contrived stress. Argument 0 is evaluated into an LLVM SSA temp, then argument 1
// runs and allocates. output_body.sf roots a local or a parameter by pushing the
// address of its alloca, but an argument value mid-call-setup has neither, so
// __gc_collect never sees it and sweeps it while it is still live.
//
// Not BUGS #63: that one was the *moving* minor collector relocating an SSA temp,
// and it closed by retiring the nursery. With no move left, the non-moving major
// collector frees the temp instead of relocating it. Retiring the nursery narrowed
// #63's window without supplying the missing root.
//
// Two controls, both clean, are recorded in the #162 entry and pin the cause:
// __gc_disable() gives 0 mismatches, and hoisting both arguments into locals gives
// 0 mismatches. The second is the shape of the fix — a local gets a root slot, an
// argument temp does not — and was the workaround before the fix landed.
//
// FIVE call forms, not one, and that is the point of the second half of this file.
// The fix's first version instrumented only the direct-call argument loop and
// left the other four broken at 44-52 mismatches out of 200 apiece; the
// direct-call assertion below passed the whole time. A regression test that
// covered one form would have certified a 20%-complete fix as done. Each form has
// its own argument loop in codegen (expr_body.sf for direct/constructor/indirect,
// methods_body.sf for instance methods and list literals), so each needs its own
// assertion — they do not share the code that was fixed.
//
// Negative control, run before landing: compiled by the pre-fix compiler this file
// SEGFAULTS rather than reporting mismatches, because Pair holds a freed Box and
// `p.a.v` dereferences it. The two-assertion version only ever counted wrong
// values. Worth knowing so a future segfault here is not read as a new bug in the
// test — the failure mode of an unrooted temp is corruption first, crash second,
// depending on what got reused.
//
// Classes, deliberately, not enums. This was found while making payload enums
// GC-collectable for #154, but it is not enum-specific and a test written with
// enums would invite the reading that #154 caused it. Classes have carried GC
// headers since long before #154.

import "@test" as Test

class Box {
    var v: Int
    fun init(v: Int) { this.v = v }
    // For the instance-method form: the receiver is evaluated before the arguments
    // and stays live across them, so it needs rooting too, not just the arguments.
    fun take_m(b: Box, ignored: Int): Int { return b.v }
}

// For the constructor form. `instance` comes back from @Pair() before any argument
// runs, so it is exposed for the full width of the argument list.
class Pair {
    var a: Box
    var b: Int
    fun init(a: Box, b: Int) { this.a = a; this.b = b }
}

// Allocates enough strings to cross the collection threshold. The return value is
// deliberately uninteresting — this exists only to collect while Box(7) is in flight.
fun churn(n: Int): Int {
    var s: String = ""
    var i: Int = 0
    while (i < n) {
        s = "x" + i.to_string()
        i = i + 1
    }
    return s.length()
}

fun take(b: Box, ignored: Int): Int {
    return b.v
}

// 200 iterations rather than one: a single call may or may not straddle a
// collection, so a one-shot version of this test would pass by luck. Counting
// mismatches instead of asserting per-iteration also makes the failure legible —
// 51 of 200 says "collections", where a bare assert would just say "wrong".
var mismatches: Int = 0
var t: Int = 0
while (t < 200) {
    var got: Int = take(Box(7), churn(300))
    if (got != 7) { mismatches = mismatches + 1 }
    t = t + 1
}

Test.assert_eq(mismatches, 0, "an argument temp survives a later argument's allocation")

// The control, in the test itself: the identical work with both arguments hoisted
// into locals must pass. If this half ever fails, the cause is not #162 and the
// diagnosis above is wrong.
var hoisted_mismatches: Int = 0
var u: Int = 0
while (u < 200) {
    var b: Box = Box(7)
    var c: Int = churn(300)
    var got2: Int = take(b, c)
    if (got2 != 7) { hoisted_mismatches = hoisted_mismatches + 1 }
    u = u + 1
}

Test.assert_eq(hoisted_mismatches, 0, "control: rooted locals are unaffected")

// The other four call forms. Same window in each: hold a fresh object in the first
// position while a later position allocates. Counted separately so a partial
// regression names the form it broke.
var recv: Box = Box(1)
var indirect_fn = fun (b: Box, ignored: Int): Int => b.v

var bad_method: Int = 0
var bad_ctor: Int = 0
var bad_indirect: Int = 0
var bad_list: Int = 0

var v: Int = 0
while (v < 200) {
    // instance method — gen_namespace_call, which also carries actor and virtual
    // dispatch, so this arm covers three receivers' worth of arms below it
    if (recv.take_m(Box(7), churn(300)) != 7) { bad_method = bad_method + 1 }

    // constructor — the @Pair() instance plus each argument
    var p: Pair = Pair(Box(7), churn(300))
    if (p.a.v != 7) { bad_ctor = bad_ctor + 1 }

    // indirect call through a closure value — the env pointer is loaded out of the
    // closure pair before the arguments run, so it is exposed just as long
    if (indirect_fn(Box(7), churn(300)) != 7) { bad_indirect = bad_indirect + 1 }

    // list literal — elements are evaluated one after another into the same list
    var l: List<Box> = [Box(7), Box(churn(300))]
    if (l[0].v != 7) { bad_list = bad_list + 1 }

    v = v + 1
}

Test.assert_eq(bad_method, 0, "instance-method argument temps are rooted")
Test.assert_eq(bad_ctor, 0, "constructor argument temps and the instance are rooted")
Test.assert_eq(bad_indirect, 0, "indirect-call argument temps are rooted")
Test.assert_eq(bad_list, 0, "list-literal element temps are rooted")

Test.summary()
